-- coa2-part6-registered-office.l4: Part 6 (A company's registered office and email address), ss 86-88B,
-- lines 1055-1108 of ukpga-2006-46.txt.  LAYER 1: the law, objectively; no point of view.
--
-- SOURCE. A plain-text rendering of the Act from legislation.gov.uk made by a converter script; it is not the
-- official text and nothing here is "verified against the official text".  Quote lines are checked by srcrefs.py.
--
-- HOW TO READ AN ANSWER (the rule of 2026-10-29, assumed, not ruled by a lawyer; see NOTES.md).
--   JUST TRUE   the text says yes.   JUST FALSE  the text says no in its own words.
--   NOTHING     the text does not decide (silent, list not closed, includes, undefined term, points outside the
--               text, limited by its own wording).  A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Seams taken as inputs: s 1097A(3)(h) "default address" (coa24), s 283 "special resolution" (coa7), s 1121 "officer in default" (coa24).
-- Every name that is not in coa-core.l4 begins with the section label it implements.

-- =========================================================================================================
-- General (ss 86, 87)
-- =========================================================================================================

-- src:1057 | 86 Duty to ensure registered office at appropriate address
-- src:1058 | (1) A company must ensure that its registered office is at all times at an appropriate address.
-- src:1072 | (2) The change takes effect upon the notice being registered by the registrar, but until the end of the period of 14 days beginning with the date on which it is registered a person may validly serve any document on the company at the address previously registered.
@ref s 86 The company's registered office (the answer other parts take as a seam) (src:1057-1072)
GIVEN `the address is the address entered in the register as the company's registered office` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 86 the company has its registered office at the address` MEANS
    IF `the address is the address entered in the register as the company's registered office`
    THEN JUST TRUE -- s 86(1) speaks of "its registered office", and s 87(2) makes the address the registrar has registered the one that counts
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the text in this range does not define "registered office" beyond s 86(1) and s 87(2), and does not say that an address which is not the registered one is not the company's registered office

-- src:1059 | (2) An address is an “appropriate address” if, in the ordinary course of events—
-- src:1060 | (a) a document addressed to the company, and delivered there by hand or by post, would be expected to come to the attention of a person acting on behalf of the company, and
-- src:1061 | (b) the delivery of documents there is capable of being recorded by the obtaining of an acknowledgement of delivery.
@ref s 86(2) "appropriate address" (src:1059-1061)
GIVEN `in the ordinary course of events a document addressed to the company, and delivered there by hand or by post, would be expected to come to the attention of a person acting on behalf of the company` IS A BOOLEAN
      `in the ordinary course of events the delivery of documents there is capable of being recorded by the obtaining of an acknowledgement of delivery` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 86(2) the address is an appropriate address` MEANS
    IF       `in the ordinary course of events a document addressed to the company, and delivered there by hand or by post, would be expected to come to the attention of a person acting on behalf of the company`
         AND `in the ordinary course of events the delivery of documents there is capable of being recorded by the obtaining of an acknowledgement of delivery`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An address is an “appropriate address” if, in the ordinary course of events— (a) ... and (b) ..."

-- src:1057 | 86 Duty to ensure registered office at appropriate address
-- src:1058 | (1) A company must ensure that its registered office is at all times at an appropriate address.
-- src:1068 | (5) Subsection (1) does not apply in relation to a company during any period for which the address of its registered office is a default address nominated by virtue of section 1097A(3)(h).
@ref s 86(1), (5) Duty to ensure registered office at appropriate address (src:1057-1068)
GIVEN `s 1097A(3)(h) the address of the registered office is a default address nominated by the registrar for the period` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 86(1) the company must ensure that its registered office is at an appropriate address` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 1097A(3)(h) the address of the registered office is a default address nominated by the registrar for the period`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subsection (1) does not apply in relation to a company during any period for which the address of its registered office is a default address nominated by virtue of section 1097A(3)(h)"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the address is a default address nominated under s 1097A(3)(h) is not known

-- src:1062 | (3) If a company fails, without reasonable excuse, to comply with this section an offence is committed by—
-- src:1063 | (a) the company, and
-- src:1064 | (b) every officer of the company who is in default.
@ref s 86(3) Offence: failure without reasonable excuse (who commits it) (src:1062-1064)
GIVEN `the company fails to comply with this section` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the company has a reasonable excuse for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN
      person IS A Party
      `s 1121 the officer is in default` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 86(3) the person commits an offence` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`the company fails to comply with this section`)) OR `the company has a reasonable excuse for the failure`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If a company fails, without reasonable excuse, to comply with this section an offence is committed by"
    ELSE CONSIDER person
         WHEN Company THEN JUST TRUE
         WHEN `Officer in default` THEN JUST TRUE
         WHEN Director THEN `liability of an officer`
         WHEN `Company secretary` THEN `liability of an officer`
         OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text names the company and every officer in default; it has no sentence treating a shadow director as an officer here
    WHERE
        `liability of an officer` MEANS
            IF `s 1121 the officer is in default` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "every officer of the company who is in default"

-- src:1065 | (4) A person guilty of an offence under this section is liable on summary conviction—
-- src:1066 | (a) in England and Wales, to a fine;
-- src:1067 | (b) in Scotland or Northern Ireland, to a fine not exceeding level 5 on the standard scale and, for continued contravention, a daily default fine not exceeding one-tenth of level 5 on the standard scale.
@ref s 86(4) Offence (penalty) (src:1065-1067)
GIVEN `the place where the person is convicted` IS A `Place of registration`
GIVETH A Penalty
`s 86(4) the penalty on summary conviction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the place where the person is convicted`
    WHEN `England and Wales` THEN
        Penalty WITH
            `how convicted` IS `on summary conviction in England and Wales`
            `imprisonment not exceeding` IS NOTHING
            `fine not exceeding` IS JUST `a fine without a stated limit`
            `daily default fine for continued contravention` IS NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): paragraph (a) says "to a fine" and mentions no daily default fine
            `imprisonment and fine together` IS FALSE
    WHEN Scotland THEN
        Penalty WITH
            `how convicted` IS `on summary conviction in Scotland`
            `imprisonment not exceeding` IS NOTHING
            `fine not exceeding` IS JUST (`a fine not exceeding a level on the standard scale` 5 1)
            `daily default fine for continued contravention` IS JUST (`a fine not exceeding a level on the standard scale` 5 0.1)
            `imprisonment and fine together` IS FALSE
    WHEN `Northern Ireland` THEN
        Penalty WITH
            `how convicted` IS `on summary conviction in Northern Ireland`
            `imprisonment not exceeding` IS NOTHING
            `fine not exceeding` IS JUST (`a fine not exceeding a level on the standard scale` 5 1)
            `daily default fine for continued contravention` IS JUST (`a fine not exceeding a level on the standard scale` 5 0.1)
            `imprisonment and fine together` IS FALSE

